1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,460
All I knew was that was a zip code that had really low rates.

2
00:00:03,460 --> 00:00:05,700
But it didn't have anything to do with rates.

3
00:00:05,700 --> 00:00:07,460
So you try that.

4
00:00:07,460 --> 00:00:12,980
So anyway, so you'll be allowed to do age.

5
00:00:12,980 --> 00:00:15,060
So generally, you are allowed to do age.

6
00:00:15,060 --> 00:00:19,500
But on the other hand, not kosher to do medical record.

7
00:00:19,500 --> 00:00:24,340
Or for auto insurance, yes, you can do age for auto insurance.

8
00:00:24,340 --> 00:00:26,140
But you cannot.

9
00:00:26,140 --> 00:00:28,420
You can do driver's record, driving record,

10
00:00:28,420 --> 00:00:30,780
but only to an extent.

11
00:00:30,780 --> 00:00:33,320
So there's a compression where there's a point where, no, no,

12
00:00:33,320 --> 00:00:36,060
just because the guy's killed 30 people already,

13
00:00:36,060 --> 00:00:37,780
if he's in jail, fine.

14
00:00:37,780 --> 00:00:39,900
But if he's free, you can't go and charge him

15
00:00:39,900 --> 00:00:42,020
a billion dollars for auto insurance.

16
00:00:42,020 --> 00:00:42,520
No.

17
00:00:42,520 --> 00:00:45,460
It's like, well, I'll probably lose money unless I do.

18
00:00:45,460 --> 00:00:46,700
That's not fair.

19
00:00:46,700 --> 00:00:49,740
So in the state of New Jersey, when I was living there,

20
00:00:49,740 --> 00:00:51,220
this whole system of assigned risks

21
00:00:51,220 --> 00:00:53,860
where basically the people that no insurance company wants

22
00:00:53,860 --> 00:00:57,700
to cover the regulated rate is just told, all right, well,

23
00:00:57,700 --> 00:01:00,020
you know all those people you don't want to cover?

24
00:01:00,020 --> 00:01:00,860
Right?

25
00:01:00,860 --> 00:01:01,360
Let's see.

26
00:01:01,360 --> 00:01:03,900
According to our math, you insure 7% of the people

27
00:01:03,900 --> 00:01:06,260
in the states, right?

28
00:01:06,260 --> 00:01:07,140
Yeah.

29
00:01:07,140 --> 00:01:07,620
All right.

30
00:01:07,620 --> 00:01:08,540
Well, guess what?

31
00:01:08,540 --> 00:01:12,180
You have to insure 7% of the riskiest drivers in New Jersey

32
00:01:12,180 --> 00:01:14,900
at a loss.

33
00:01:14,900 --> 00:01:16,500
Well, at least it's not all of them.

34
00:01:16,500 --> 00:01:17,000
All right.

35
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:20,540
So that kind of a system.

36
00:01:20,540 --> 00:01:23,140
And again, so you're like, but I don't want to.

37
00:01:23,140 --> 00:01:23,940
Well, look.

38
00:01:23,940 --> 00:01:26,900
Well, you can charge them double the normal rate.

39
00:01:26,900 --> 00:01:27,740
Well, thanks a lot.

40
00:01:27,740 --> 00:01:29,900
Do you want to insure them at double the normal rate?

41
00:01:29,900 --> 00:01:31,140
And I don't.

42
00:01:31,140 --> 00:01:33,660
I might consider like 50 times the normal rate.

43
00:01:33,660 --> 00:01:35,580
Double the normal rate isn't even beginning

44
00:01:35,580 --> 00:01:38,580
to get anywhere in the ballpark.

45
00:01:38,580 --> 00:01:42,700
And then how about conditioning insurance premium on sex?

46
00:01:42,700 --> 00:01:44,900
Is that OK?

47
00:01:44,900 --> 00:01:46,860
It depends a lot.

48
00:01:46,860 --> 00:01:47,380
Right?

49
00:01:47,380 --> 00:01:50,420
And a cynic would say, well, if men are higher risk,

50
00:01:50,420 --> 00:01:51,900
then it's OK.

51
00:01:51,900 --> 00:01:52,400
Right?

52
00:01:52,400 --> 00:01:55,060
Like on auto insurance, men have higher risk.

53
00:01:55,060 --> 00:01:57,700
And I guess anyone here who's a male driver

54
00:01:57,700 --> 00:02:00,140
and you were charged what seemed like exorbitant rates when

55
00:02:00,140 --> 00:02:02,220
you were a teenager, or your parents were charged

56
00:02:02,220 --> 00:02:03,500
the exorbitant rates.

57
00:02:03,500 --> 00:02:06,580
And then any decent economists say, yeah, well,

58
00:02:06,580 --> 00:02:08,980
males are on average really dangerous drivers.

59
00:02:08,980 --> 00:02:09,480
Right?

60
00:02:09,480 --> 00:02:11,660
And suppose that as a 16-year-old male driver,

61
00:02:11,660 --> 00:02:14,980
you would say, well, I've never been in an accident.

62
00:02:14,980 --> 00:02:18,580
It's not fair that I get charged more than a woman who's

63
00:02:18,580 --> 00:02:20,260
not been in an accident.

64
00:02:20,260 --> 00:02:22,500
And what is the honest statistical answer

65
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:24,820
that a company probably doesn't want to tell you about?

66
00:02:24,820 --> 00:02:25,580
What's the truth?

67
00:02:25,580 --> 00:02:26,500
It's coming.

68
00:02:26,500 --> 00:02:27,000
It's coming.

69
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:28,900
It's coming.

70
00:02:28,900 --> 00:02:29,820
Probably.

71
00:02:29,820 --> 00:02:32,700
Probably coming.

72
00:02:32,700 --> 00:02:36,500
There is always this individual level of justice of, look,

73
00:02:36,500 --> 00:02:39,380
you don't know it, but I happen to be an incredibly safe male

74
00:02:39,380 --> 00:02:39,880
driver.

75
00:02:39,880 --> 00:02:41,660
It's like, well, good for you.

76
00:02:41,660 --> 00:02:43,620
In 10 years, I'll believe you.

77
00:02:43,620 --> 00:02:47,100
But first, I'm going to, when essentially you

78
00:02:47,100 --> 00:02:49,020
have no track record, then you're

79
00:02:49,020 --> 00:02:51,780
going to base your view on general statistics.

80
00:02:51,780 --> 00:02:53,580
And then as your track record accumulates,

81
00:02:53,580 --> 00:02:56,100
you'll put more and more weight on the track record.

82
00:02:56,100 --> 00:02:58,460
Although, if it's for a very rare event,

83
00:02:58,460 --> 00:03:01,300
then you might continue to put a little weight on the track

84
00:03:01,300 --> 00:03:03,660
record, just because rare events usually don't happen,

85
00:03:03,660 --> 00:03:06,260
even for high-risk people.

86
00:03:06,260 --> 00:03:09,780
So if there's never been a nuclear accident

87
00:03:09,780 --> 00:03:12,740
in your country, they say, hey, we got a perfect success rating.

88
00:03:12,740 --> 00:03:15,460
So insurance should basically be free.

89
00:03:15,460 --> 00:03:18,460
Yeah, but you wouldn't expect Belgium

90
00:03:18,460 --> 00:03:20,980
to have had any accidents statistically.

91
00:03:20,980 --> 00:03:23,980
But nevertheless, the expected loss

92
00:03:23,980 --> 00:03:27,420
is high enough that it's going to be a high rate.

93
00:03:27,420 --> 00:03:28,780
So you've got all the regulations

94
00:03:28,780 --> 00:03:31,060
that forbid insurers from conditioning premium

95
00:03:31,060 --> 00:03:33,260
on biocharacteristics, which again,

96
00:03:33,260 --> 00:03:37,020
is directly contradictory to the idea that companies are like,

97
00:03:37,020 --> 00:03:39,100
I don't know how risky anybody is.

98
00:03:39,100 --> 00:03:40,140
Poor me.

99
00:03:40,140 --> 00:03:42,380
Instead, this is the insurance companies

100
00:03:42,380 --> 00:03:44,180
do know how risky people are.

101
00:03:44,180 --> 00:03:45,540
And if we let them, they're going

102
00:03:45,540 --> 00:03:47,140
to charge high rates to risky people

103
00:03:47,140 --> 00:03:49,220
and low rates to safe people.

104
00:03:49,220 --> 00:03:51,980
And this is, of course, the very thing

105
00:03:51,980 --> 00:03:53,500
that you would do if you didn't want

106
00:03:53,500 --> 00:03:56,460
there to be adverse selection.

107
00:03:56,460 --> 00:03:59,180
Now, what does a normal person actually think?

108
00:03:59,180 --> 00:04:01,380
It's one thing to go and hear about adverse selection

109
00:04:01,380 --> 00:04:04,300
and then say, oh, that's why this stuff is good.

110
00:04:04,300 --> 00:04:07,180
But if you just were to go and talk to your grandmom

111
00:04:07,180 --> 00:04:09,740
or something like that and say, isn't it terrible

112
00:04:09,740 --> 00:04:12,780
that you can't charge risky people high rates?

113
00:04:12,780 --> 00:04:15,140
What is a normal human reaction to an insurance company

114
00:04:15,140 --> 00:04:17,980
charging a risky person a high rate?

115
00:04:17,980 --> 00:04:18,700
It's not fair.

116
00:04:18,700 --> 00:04:19,380
You're mean.

117
00:04:19,380 --> 00:04:20,380
You're greedy.

118
00:04:20,380 --> 00:04:23,180
It's like, well, why aren't you greedy for not offering

119
00:04:23,180 --> 00:04:25,380
to insure people to loss?

120
00:04:25,380 --> 00:04:27,660
Why is it that just because I call myself an insurance

121
00:04:27,660 --> 00:04:31,500
company, I'm expected to throw money to total strangers?

122
00:04:31,500 --> 00:04:33,060
But that comes with the territory.

123
00:04:33,060 --> 00:04:34,700
If you want to be an insurance company,

124
00:04:34,700 --> 00:04:37,340
you have to lose money on some bad risks.

125
00:04:37,340 --> 00:04:39,020
That's the expectation.

126
00:04:39,020 --> 00:04:43,020
Now, again, I will say that if your high-cost people are

127
00:04:43,020 --> 00:04:45,820
kids with leukemia, I at least understand

128
00:04:45,820 --> 00:04:47,260
the fairness intuition.

129
00:04:47,260 --> 00:04:51,540
If the high-risk people are bad drivers in New Jersey,

130
00:04:51,540 --> 00:04:53,140
this is where I just completely lose

131
00:04:53,140 --> 00:04:55,060
contact with what normal people are thinking.

132
00:04:55,060 --> 00:04:57,460
Like, what are you talking about?

133
00:04:57,460 --> 00:04:59,540
You want them to be driving?

134
00:04:59,540 --> 00:05:01,940
Wouldn't it be best if they had to pay really high rates

135
00:05:01,940 --> 00:05:05,020
and therefore stop driving?

136
00:05:05,020 --> 00:05:08,340
And then this is where someone like my dad,

137
00:05:08,340 --> 00:05:10,940
who has a lifelong love affair with the automobile,

138
00:05:10,940 --> 00:05:14,620
will make an argument that makes a lot of sense to people,

139
00:05:14,620 --> 00:05:17,580
although it seems completely erasing me as a Brian.

140
00:05:17,580 --> 00:05:20,620
People have to drive.

141
00:05:20,620 --> 00:05:22,100
Don't you understand?

142
00:05:22,100 --> 00:05:24,380
People have to drive.

143
00:05:24,380 --> 00:05:26,500
And since they have to drive, it's

144
00:05:26,500 --> 00:05:30,740
not fair to go and charge that much money to Captain Death

145
00:05:30,740 --> 00:05:32,820
behind the wheel.

146
00:05:32,820 --> 00:05:35,540
Captain Death has to drive.

147
00:05:35,540 --> 00:05:37,780
Has he referred to Captain Death directly?

148
00:05:37,780 --> 00:05:39,660
Or is this just?

149
00:05:39,660 --> 00:05:40,940
I think I just made him up now.

150
00:05:40,940 --> 00:05:41,440
Yeah.

151
00:05:44,300 --> 00:05:45,940
Still, I will say this seems very odd.

152
00:05:45,940 --> 00:05:48,540
It's like, look, if he lost his license,

153
00:05:48,540 --> 00:05:52,020
then the I have to drive excuse wouldn't count.

154
00:05:52,020 --> 00:05:54,060
And you wouldn't mind jailing him for that

155
00:05:54,060 --> 00:05:56,180
if Captain Death kept driving around

156
00:05:56,180 --> 00:05:58,100
without a license after a vote.

157
00:05:58,100 --> 00:06:01,980
It's like, well, if you're sentenced in a court of law,

158
00:06:01,980 --> 00:06:05,860
then the I have to drive defense is overruled.

159
00:06:05,860 --> 00:06:08,180
But if it's just a matter of economic incentives

160
00:06:08,180 --> 00:06:10,900
that no one wants to insure you, then that's fine.

161
00:06:10,900 --> 00:06:13,580
And probably a better story would just be, well,

162
00:06:13,580 --> 00:06:15,540
if they don't go, if you charge them too much,

163
00:06:15,540 --> 00:06:16,780
they'll just drive without insurance.

164
00:06:16,780 --> 00:06:18,380
And we're worried about all the people

165
00:06:18,380 --> 00:06:20,140
that are victimized without the insurance.

166
00:06:20,140 --> 00:06:22,460
But again, you might say, well, how about you

167
00:06:22,460 --> 00:06:24,500
get 30 years in jail if you get in an accident

168
00:06:24,500 --> 00:06:26,140
after driving without insurance?

169
00:06:26,140 --> 00:06:29,380
We could do that instead of making insurance companies

170
00:06:29,380 --> 00:06:32,820
go and give heavily subsidized insurance to high-risk people,

171
00:06:32,820 --> 00:06:35,540
which causes which one of these two things?

172
00:06:35,540 --> 00:06:37,020
Does that?

173
00:06:37,020 --> 00:06:38,660
Actually, it's actually both.

174
00:06:38,660 --> 00:06:40,380
Yes, but the moral hazard as well.

175
00:06:40,380 --> 00:06:42,260
People that would have given up on driving

176
00:06:42,260 --> 00:06:46,220
because the rates are too high will then keep doing it.

177
00:06:46,220 --> 00:06:47,300
And then we'll see.

178
00:06:47,300 --> 00:06:49,720
I'm not sure, but I suspect that when you start getting up

179
00:06:49,720 --> 00:06:51,260
to very high ages, then there's also

180
00:06:51,260 --> 00:06:53,460
some regulation about what is the highest premium you

181
00:06:53,460 --> 00:06:56,020
can charge an 80-year-old.

182
00:06:56,020 --> 00:06:57,900
And again, on the one hand, we don't

183
00:06:57,900 --> 00:06:59,020
want 80-year-olds driving.

184
00:06:59,020 --> 00:07:01,140
On the other hand, we don't want them overcharged.

185
00:07:01,140 --> 00:07:05,620
So it's a tough situation for people

186
00:07:05,620 --> 00:07:10,780
with a clash of their populist intuitions.

187
00:07:10,780 --> 00:07:12,500
So anyway, there's regulations that

188
00:07:12,500 --> 00:07:14,200
forbid insurers from conditioning premium

189
00:07:14,200 --> 00:07:16,140
on buyer characteristics.

190
00:07:16,140 --> 00:07:18,940
There is subsidies for car insurance for reckless drivers

191
00:07:18,940 --> 00:07:20,860
or forcing insurers to cover their loss.

192
00:07:20,860 --> 00:07:22,580
Sometimes the state just pays a subsidy

193
00:07:22,580 --> 00:07:24,980
to go and make the insurance affordable for the reckless

194
00:07:24,980 --> 00:07:26,620
drivers.

195
00:07:26,620 --> 00:07:28,380
Reckless is such a harsh word.

196
00:07:28,380 --> 00:07:32,220
Let's just say multiple accident drivers,

197
00:07:32,220 --> 00:07:35,860
habitual accident-prone drivers.

198
00:07:35,860 --> 00:07:37,060
Doesn't mean you're reckless.

199
00:07:37,060 --> 00:07:38,940
Maybe you're just a bad driver.

200
00:07:38,940 --> 00:07:40,620
You're being very cautious, but you just

201
00:07:40,620 --> 00:07:43,420
keep ramming into people.

202
00:07:43,420 --> 00:07:47,100
And then, of course, there's the ban on free musician clauses.

203
00:07:47,100 --> 00:07:49,380
This has been properly ridiculed, especially

204
00:07:49,380 --> 00:07:50,700
for life insurance.

205
00:07:50,700 --> 00:07:52,460
It's like, well, why is it you have

206
00:07:52,460 --> 00:07:54,420
to buy life insurance while you're still alive?

207
00:07:54,420 --> 00:07:57,100
Why can't the dead buy it?

208
00:07:57,100 --> 00:07:59,820
And of course, if presumably your heirs

209
00:07:59,820 --> 00:08:03,660
would go and buy life insurance for you after your death,

210
00:08:03,660 --> 00:08:05,700
what would be an obvious thing to do as soon

211
00:08:05,700 --> 00:08:09,140
as your beloved relatives pass away?

212
00:08:09,140 --> 00:08:11,020
Or head right down to the insurance companies

213
00:08:11,020 --> 00:08:14,820
and say, I would like to have a billion dollars of life

214
00:08:14,820 --> 00:08:16,180
insurance.

215
00:08:16,180 --> 00:08:18,460
And of course, if this were really going on,

216
00:08:18,460 --> 00:08:21,980
then remember when we were talking about how no company

217
00:08:21,980 --> 00:08:24,180
really will be able to sell insurance at an actually fair

218
00:08:24,180 --> 00:08:25,820
rate because they'd be losing money,

219
00:08:25,820 --> 00:08:27,380
because they're investing over overhead.

220
00:08:27,380 --> 00:08:29,540
So then the rates would be even more than a billion

221
00:08:29,540 --> 00:08:32,380
dollar policy, and it would destroy the markets.

222
00:08:32,380 --> 00:08:34,220
So that's a particularly fair case.

223
00:08:34,220 --> 00:08:37,700
But any of these cases do basically follow.

224
00:08:41,060 --> 00:08:45,380
So questions about the real world of regulation.

225
00:08:45,380 --> 00:08:48,740
So much of it which does seem to be specifically concerned

226
00:08:48,740 --> 00:08:51,620
with amplifying the two problems that economists usually

227
00:08:51,620 --> 00:08:54,860
use to justify the problem.

228
00:08:54,860 --> 00:08:56,780
Now, by the way, it is possible, of course,

229
00:08:56,780 --> 00:08:59,060
that insurance companies can have a lot of information,

230
00:08:59,060 --> 00:09:01,260
but there's still some residual after selection.

231
00:09:01,260 --> 00:09:03,300
Like it could be that insurance companies

232
00:09:03,300 --> 00:09:05,740
can make a very good prediction of your risk

233
00:09:05,740 --> 00:09:10,060
conditional upon your age, sex, occupation, location,

234
00:09:10,060 --> 00:09:12,220
a bunch of other factors.

235
00:09:12,220 --> 00:09:15,700
Health history, of course, is another really good one to use.

236
00:09:15,700 --> 00:09:18,260
And yet, you could still have a little bit

237
00:09:18,260 --> 00:09:22,100
of additional information on top of all the information

238
00:09:22,100 --> 00:09:24,020
in the database of the insurance company.

239
00:09:24,020 --> 00:09:25,620
That's going to be a marginal problem,

240
00:09:25,620 --> 00:09:27,620
but there could still be a little bit of problem

241
00:09:27,620 --> 00:09:28,940
left on top.

242
00:09:28,940 --> 00:09:34,780
But again, do not underestimate the amazing power of actuaries

243
00:09:34,780 --> 00:09:37,420
and how much they know.

244
00:09:37,420 --> 00:09:39,140
By the way, actuary is a great profession

245
00:09:39,140 --> 00:09:42,060
if you like taking tests.

246
00:09:42,060 --> 00:09:43,580
So my best friend at Princeton did

247
00:09:43,580 --> 00:09:45,020
economic consulting for two years

248
00:09:45,020 --> 00:09:46,380
and said this is terrible.

249
00:09:46,380 --> 00:09:48,260
I hate every second of this.

250
00:09:48,260 --> 00:09:51,060
And then he was the math guru in our program.

251
00:09:51,060 --> 00:09:53,460
So he was the one who we would always go to with our math.

252
00:09:53,460 --> 00:09:56,700
And so he was able to go and pass the first actuarial test

253
00:09:56,700 --> 00:09:57,900
with almost no work.

254
00:09:57,900 --> 00:10:00,020
And then he just became an actuary.

255
00:10:00,020 --> 00:10:03,420
And then the tests were so easy for him.

256
00:10:03,420 --> 00:10:06,060
And the way that it works is there's seven tests

257
00:10:06,060 --> 00:10:07,620
to go and complete the program.

258
00:10:07,620 --> 00:10:09,380
And for each test, a company will generally

259
00:10:09,380 --> 00:10:10,860
give you two or three weeks vacation

260
00:10:10,860 --> 00:10:12,500
to study for the test.

261
00:10:12,500 --> 00:10:14,540
Since my friend didn't actually need to study,

262
00:10:14,540 --> 00:10:18,940
he just got six extra free paid vacations.

263
00:10:18,940 --> 00:10:21,780
So he would fly to Europe for a couple weeks

264
00:10:21,780 --> 00:10:24,300
and then come back and be like, no, I'm ready for this test.

265
00:10:24,300 --> 00:10:27,020
It's going to be real hard.

266
00:10:27,020 --> 00:10:29,700
Most people, in fact, few people on Earth

267
00:10:29,700 --> 00:10:34,500
would be at his level, which is why he was helping all of us.

268
00:10:34,500 --> 00:10:36,080
And he's a great job if you want to have

269
00:10:36,080 --> 00:10:39,500
an upper middle class income and only work 35 hours a week

270
00:10:39,500 --> 00:10:40,700
for the rest of your life.

271
00:10:40,700 --> 00:10:41,500
Retire at 60.

272
00:10:44,220 --> 00:10:45,660
He's never considered it exciting.

273
00:10:45,660 --> 00:10:48,100
But it does leave an enormous amount of time

274
00:10:48,100 --> 00:10:50,900
for his many exciting hobbies.

275
00:10:50,900 --> 00:10:53,540
This point, well, it was a good backup plan, anyway,

276
00:10:53,540 --> 00:10:56,700
for undergraduates.

277
00:10:56,700 --> 00:10:58,100
He finished his PhD at Princeton.

278
00:10:58,100 --> 00:11:02,820
He's like, wow, that didn't do me any good at all.

279
00:11:02,820 --> 00:11:03,460
OK.

280
00:11:03,460 --> 00:11:05,260
Now, there has been some empirical work

281
00:11:05,260 --> 00:11:07,220
on the severity of adverse selection.

282
00:11:07,220 --> 00:11:10,180
And here's this frightening thing.

283
00:11:10,180 --> 00:11:12,580
At the time that this was being invoked

284
00:11:12,580 --> 00:11:17,220
as a ironclad argument in favor of Obamacare,

285
00:11:17,220 --> 00:11:19,060
there were already some published papers

286
00:11:19,060 --> 00:11:20,860
saying that adverse selection doesn't even

287
00:11:20,860 --> 00:11:22,700
seem to be much of a problem.

288
00:11:22,700 --> 00:11:25,260
It's not even clear that it exists.

289
00:11:25,260 --> 00:11:28,020
Or maybe there is reverse adverse selection,

290
00:11:28,020 --> 00:11:29,100
as we'll see.

291
00:11:29,100 --> 00:11:29,980
All right.

292
00:11:29,980 --> 00:11:32,460
So anyway, so two takes on why there's

293
00:11:32,460 --> 00:11:34,580
so little evidence for adverse selection,

294
00:11:34,580 --> 00:11:38,020
except in cases where regulation causes adverse selection.

295
00:11:38,020 --> 00:11:39,660
So there are studies of cases where

296
00:11:39,660 --> 00:11:41,900
you have extreme regulations, which

297
00:11:41,900 --> 00:11:43,380
do cause adverse selection.

298
00:11:43,380 --> 00:11:45,380
But that's, of course, not the kind of evidence

299
00:11:45,380 --> 00:11:48,180
that some advocate of a market failure theory is looking for.

300
00:11:48,180 --> 00:11:50,220
You want to find an unregulated market where

301
00:11:50,220 --> 00:11:52,260
the market has been destroyed.

302
00:11:52,260 --> 00:11:55,780
Anyway, so two different takes.

303
00:11:55,780 --> 00:11:58,020
So one of them is that insurance companies actually

304
00:11:58,020 --> 00:12:00,340
know more about you than you do.

305
00:12:00,340 --> 00:12:03,380
So if an actuary disagrees with your self-assessment

306
00:12:03,380 --> 00:12:05,100
of your own health or your own risk,

307
00:12:05,100 --> 00:12:07,220
maybe you should just leave the actuary,

308
00:12:07,220 --> 00:12:08,900
throw away what you think you know.

309
00:12:08,900 --> 00:12:10,940
So they have these actuary tables, and you don't.

310
00:12:10,940 --> 00:12:12,440
Of course, they're no longer tables.

311
00:12:12,440 --> 00:12:16,860
Now it's a giant, incredibly sophisticated computer model.

312
00:12:16,860 --> 00:12:20,780
And then another story, this is getting a little bit ourselves,

313
00:12:20,780 --> 00:12:25,220
but there's been a fair bit of work on personality economics.

314
00:12:25,220 --> 00:12:29,720
So one of the main traits that is studied in personality

315
00:12:29,720 --> 00:12:32,940
psychology is what they call conscientiousness.

316
00:12:32,940 --> 00:12:35,900
Or if you know your Myers-Briggs personality type,

317
00:12:35,900 --> 00:12:39,060
this is the same as J versus P, where

318
00:12:39,060 --> 00:12:42,220
J equals high conscientiousness and P

319
00:12:42,220 --> 00:12:45,100
equals low conscientiousness.

320
00:12:45,100 --> 00:12:46,540
Why that?

321
00:12:46,540 --> 00:12:49,940
Well, Myers-Briggs is generally given in workplaces

322
00:12:49,940 --> 00:12:52,180
where they want to have the story of we all

323
00:12:52,180 --> 00:12:54,780
work best as a team and we need all types, especially

324
00:12:54,780 --> 00:12:55,460
lazy people.

325
00:12:55,460 --> 00:12:58,580
That's what they call them.

326
00:12:58,580 --> 00:12:59,820
Officially late people.

327
00:12:59,820 --> 00:13:01,480
Anyway, so they didn't want to say that.

328
00:13:01,480 --> 00:13:04,020
So instead they gave an obscurantist name

329
00:13:04,020 --> 00:13:06,740
to low conscientiousness, which is perceiving.

330
00:13:06,740 --> 00:13:09,340
And so anyway, if anyone here is perceiving,

331
00:13:09,340 --> 00:13:11,620
many of my favorite people in the world are perceiving.

332
00:13:11,620 --> 00:13:12,420
They're my favorite friends.

333
00:13:12,420 --> 00:13:13,840
They're not my favorite employees.

334
00:13:13,840 --> 00:13:16,460
No, I'm just kidding.

335
00:13:16,460 --> 00:13:18,340
All right, in any case, so there's

336
00:13:18,340 --> 00:13:20,800
this writing conscientiousness that's been heavily studied

337
00:13:20,800 --> 00:13:21,660
in psychology.

338
00:13:21,660 --> 00:13:26,060
And basic idea is people are high in conscientiousness.

339
00:13:26,060 --> 00:13:27,180
They are hardworking.

340
00:13:27,180 --> 00:13:31,700
They are disciplined, orderly, punctual, neat, tidy, boring,

341
00:13:31,700 --> 00:13:32,940
all those things.

342
00:13:32,940 --> 00:13:34,540
And on the other hand, you have people

343
00:13:34,540 --> 00:13:40,940
who are low in conscientiousness who are thrill-seeking, lazy,

344
00:13:40,940 --> 00:13:46,740
and disorganized, and messy, sloppy, living in pigsty.

345
00:13:46,740 --> 00:13:48,020
But fun.

346
00:13:48,020 --> 00:13:49,780
So anyway, we got these.

347
00:13:49,780 --> 00:13:53,700
Now, once you accept this as just a general difference

348
00:13:53,700 --> 00:13:57,180
between human beings, then you might think, hmm,

349
00:13:57,180 --> 00:14:00,340
what kind of people do you think buy lots of insurance?

350
00:14:00,340 --> 00:14:01,820
Yeah, conscientious people.

351
00:14:01,820 --> 00:14:04,500
And how many accidents do conscientious people generally

352
00:14:04,500 --> 00:14:05,000
have?

353
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:05,500
Less.

354
00:14:05,500 --> 00:14:06,980
No, yeah, they don't have many.

355
00:14:06,980 --> 00:14:10,980
All right, so there's many different amusing tests

356
00:14:10,980 --> 00:14:11,940
of this.

357
00:14:11,940 --> 00:14:15,900
So one of them, so people who drive motorcycles

358
00:14:15,900 --> 00:14:17,420
without helmets.

359
00:14:17,420 --> 00:14:21,460
So and even in states where insurance companies

360
00:14:21,460 --> 00:14:24,300
were required to sell them insurance at the same price

361
00:14:24,300 --> 00:14:26,700
as someone who wasn't a motorcycle rider.

362
00:14:26,700 --> 00:14:28,420
All right, so do you think the people who

363
00:14:28,420 --> 00:14:30,340
ended up in hospitals because they had accidents where

364
00:14:30,340 --> 00:14:33,380
they weren't wearing a helmet, are they generally loaded up

365
00:14:33,380 --> 00:14:36,500
to the gills with health insurance?

366
00:14:36,500 --> 00:14:37,700
No, they are not.

367
00:14:37,700 --> 00:14:39,300
They are more likely to be uninsured.

368
00:14:39,300 --> 00:14:41,300
They're doing something incredibly risky.

369
00:14:41,300 --> 00:14:43,500
So in a simple adverse selection model,

370
00:14:43,500 --> 00:14:45,140
they would have tons of insurance.

371
00:14:45,140 --> 00:14:48,460
Instead, they are very likely to be uninsured.

372
00:14:48,460 --> 00:14:50,740
So growing up in the state of California,

373
00:14:50,740 --> 00:14:52,300
very strong conventional wisdom, which

374
00:14:52,300 --> 00:14:54,540
is confirmed that every time my parents were involved

375
00:14:54,540 --> 00:14:56,660
in an auto accident, is the person

376
00:14:56,660 --> 00:15:01,420
that you get in an accident with will not have auto insurance.

377
00:15:01,420 --> 00:15:02,660
You may say, that's odd.

378
00:15:02,660 --> 00:15:05,740
Shouldn't accident-prone people have lots of auto insurance?

379
00:15:05,740 --> 00:15:07,140
Well, the same kind of people who

380
00:15:07,140 --> 00:15:09,380
don't look over their shoulder into their blind spot

381
00:15:09,380 --> 00:15:12,060
before they change lanes also don't really

382
00:15:12,060 --> 00:15:13,620
worry that much about whether they're

383
00:15:13,620 --> 00:15:15,580
going to have insurance.

384
00:15:15,580 --> 00:15:16,420
There's another one.

385
00:15:16,420 --> 00:15:18,160
So have you ever had a credit card company

386
00:15:18,160 --> 00:15:22,300
try to sell you extra protection against theft

387
00:15:22,300 --> 00:15:24,340
of your credit card?

388
00:15:24,340 --> 00:15:24,940
All right.

389
00:15:24,940 --> 00:15:28,100
Now, what do you think about whether this

390
00:15:28,100 --> 00:15:29,180
is a good policy to buy?

391
00:15:29,180 --> 00:15:30,900
So let me just give you a hint.

392
00:15:30,900 --> 00:15:34,140
Legally, you're generally only liable for $50

393
00:15:34,140 --> 00:15:36,460
of stolen credit card charges as long as you

394
00:15:36,460 --> 00:15:38,540
report it within the day.

395
00:15:38,540 --> 00:15:42,620
So you're paying, you might be paying like $25 a year

396
00:15:42,620 --> 00:15:45,660
in order to avoid a maximum loss of $50.

397
00:15:45,660 --> 00:15:47,180
Not very good, but anyway.

398
00:15:47,180 --> 00:15:49,420
So there was some researcher who looked at this

399
00:15:49,420 --> 00:15:52,420
and found out, out of people that do buy this,

400
00:15:52,420 --> 00:15:54,820
do you think they have a high rate of losing their wallets

401
00:15:54,820 --> 00:15:55,820
or a low rate?

402
00:15:55,820 --> 00:15:56,320
A low rate.

403
00:15:56,320 --> 00:15:57,500
They have a low rate.

404
00:15:57,500 --> 00:16:00,380
Again, not what you would expect.

405
00:16:00,380 --> 00:16:02,740
Now, the, so let's see.

406
00:16:02,740 --> 00:16:06,180
So again, probably one of the very best studies in this.

407
00:16:06,180 --> 00:16:08,100
So in the AR, there's a study of life insurance

408
00:16:08,100 --> 00:16:10,100
to see whether there's adverse selection there.

409
00:16:10,100 --> 00:16:12,860
And the basic prediction of the adverse selection model

410
00:16:12,860 --> 00:16:16,660
is people who have a higher risk of death

411
00:16:16,660 --> 00:16:19,020
are going to have more or less insurance.

412
00:16:19,020 --> 00:16:19,940
More.

413
00:16:19,940 --> 00:16:23,580
What is very strongly shown in the data?

414
00:16:23,580 --> 00:16:25,460
Exactly the opposite.

415
00:16:25,460 --> 00:16:28,860
So what kinds of people are loaded up to the gills

416
00:16:28,860 --> 00:16:30,860
with life insurance?

417
00:16:30,860 --> 00:16:32,100
People like me.

418
00:16:32,100 --> 00:16:35,620
All right, so like the most dangerous thing I do is vlog.

419
00:16:35,620 --> 00:16:37,900
Right?

420
00:16:37,900 --> 00:16:39,340
And if anything bad happens to me,

421
00:16:39,340 --> 00:16:41,300
I probably will have something to do with that.

422
00:16:41,300 --> 00:16:43,820
But I think I'm still really dangerous.

423
00:16:43,820 --> 00:16:46,700
So people who have lots of life insurance

424
00:16:46,700 --> 00:16:52,140
tend to be people who have very safe jobs, very secure incomes.

425
00:16:52,140 --> 00:16:54,100
So they've got kids.

426
00:16:54,100 --> 00:16:56,300
They're married.

427
00:16:56,300 --> 00:16:58,500
So what are they, every weekend, what are they doing?

428
00:16:58,500 --> 00:17:00,220
Staying home with the kids.

429
00:17:00,220 --> 00:17:02,140
So this kind of lifestyle.

430
00:17:02,140 --> 00:17:05,580
And so how much supplemental life insurance do you think I

431
00:17:05,580 --> 00:17:07,620
have?

432
00:17:07,620 --> 00:17:09,100
My wife has a rule.

433
00:17:09,100 --> 00:17:11,420
We each have to have enough supplemental life insurance

434
00:17:11,420 --> 00:17:14,020
to buy our entire house outright.

435
00:17:14,020 --> 00:17:17,900
So we need to have supplemental life insurance equal to the

436
00:17:17,900 --> 00:17:19,820
entire value of our mortgage.

437
00:17:19,820 --> 00:17:21,660
Now, I don't know where this rule came from.

438
00:17:21,660 --> 00:17:25,580
But anyway, this is our approach.

439
00:17:25,580 --> 00:17:28,220
But anyway, so people like me tend

440
00:17:28,220 --> 00:17:30,260
to have a whole lot of life insurance,

441
00:17:30,260 --> 00:17:32,940
despite the fact that we are very safe

442
00:17:32,940 --> 00:17:35,420
and live cautiously in the extreme.

443
00:17:35,420 --> 00:17:37,340
And again, on the other hand, people

444
00:17:37,340 --> 00:17:41,220
who are doing high-risk lifestyles generally

445
00:17:41,220 --> 00:17:44,020
are not loaded up with life insurance.

446
00:17:44,020 --> 00:17:46,500
They might not have any supplemental life insurance

447
00:17:46,500 --> 00:17:47,620
at all.

448
00:17:47,620 --> 00:17:51,780
They might not even have a job that includes life insurance.

449
00:17:51,780 --> 00:17:55,660
So this was a paper that really showed an entire industry

450
00:17:55,660 --> 00:17:58,140
where people assumed that adverse selection was basically

451
00:17:58,140 --> 00:17:58,620
there.

452
00:17:58,620 --> 00:18:00,480
Not only is there no sign of it, the evidence

453
00:18:00,480 --> 00:18:01,540
all goes the other way.

454
00:18:01,540 --> 00:18:02,920
So the only thing you could really

455
00:18:02,920 --> 00:18:04,780
do to save the model in this case

456
00:18:04,780 --> 00:18:06,580
is to say there's still adverse selection,

457
00:18:06,580 --> 00:18:07,940
but we just got the side.

458
00:18:07,940 --> 00:18:10,780
We were wrong about which side has it.

459
00:18:10,780 --> 00:18:13,540
You could say that the real problem is that insurance

460
00:18:13,540 --> 00:18:16,580
companies are the ones with more information than you.

461
00:18:16,580 --> 00:18:18,180
And then people are not buying it

462
00:18:18,180 --> 00:18:19,740
because when an insurance company tries

463
00:18:19,740 --> 00:18:21,500
to sell you insurance, they're like, hmm,

464
00:18:21,500 --> 00:18:22,880
well, you don't think you're trying to sell it to me

465
00:18:22,880 --> 00:18:25,940
if it were a bad deal, so I don't want it.

466
00:18:25,940 --> 00:18:28,660
So I haven't heard anyone actually make this argument,

467
00:18:28,660 --> 00:18:30,380
although in terms of the data, I'd

468
00:18:30,380 --> 00:18:35,420
say this is a much better story than the usual one.

469
00:18:35,420 --> 00:18:37,540
Anyway.

470
00:18:37,540 --> 00:18:38,900
All right.

471
00:18:38,900 --> 00:18:41,820
So I would say that there's still

472
00:18:41,820 --> 00:18:45,540
been shockingly little done on the story, which, again,

473
00:18:45,540 --> 00:18:47,540
is super intuitive.

474
00:18:47,540 --> 00:18:49,100
Conscientious people buy insurance,

475
00:18:49,100 --> 00:18:50,340
and they have little risks.

476
00:18:50,340 --> 00:18:54,540
And so this is at least a big offsetting factor,

477
00:18:54,540 --> 00:18:58,460
which leads us to really not see very much adverse selection.

478
00:18:58,460 --> 00:19:00,420
And then there was a paper in the Rand Journal

479
00:19:00,420 --> 00:19:02,620
that theoretically models this.

480
00:19:02,620 --> 00:19:06,220
And it's sometimes called an advantageous selection

481
00:19:06,220 --> 00:19:08,500
or propitious selection.

482
00:19:08,500 --> 00:19:11,540
So basically, it just flips them all around.

483
00:19:14,580 --> 00:19:19,740
And there's one other fun example of fun application

484
00:19:19,740 --> 00:19:22,100
of adverse selection.

485
00:19:22,100 --> 00:19:24,100
So if you're here at Mason, you may

486
00:19:24,100 --> 00:19:26,700
have heard someone talking about the wonders of integral

487
00:19:26,700 --> 00:19:29,140
capitalism at some point and abolishing the government

488
00:19:29,140 --> 00:19:31,860
and privatizing police and all this stuff.

489
00:19:31,860 --> 00:19:33,500
All right, so anyway.

490
00:19:33,500 --> 00:19:36,820
Now, whatever you think about this,

491
00:19:36,820 --> 00:19:40,820
here is one argument that people have made against it.

492
00:19:40,820 --> 00:19:44,300
And the argument says, look, if there were just

493
00:19:44,300 --> 00:19:49,820
a private police market, then what would happen

494
00:19:49,820 --> 00:19:53,060
is that companies would try to attract customers by saying,

495
00:19:53,060 --> 00:19:54,100
we'll pay for anyone.

496
00:19:54,100 --> 00:19:55,400
We don't care what you've done.

497
00:19:55,400 --> 00:19:58,020
We'll stand by you no matter what your crimes are.

498
00:19:58,020 --> 00:19:59,860
Now, in terms of the adverse selection model,

499
00:19:59,860 --> 00:20:02,020
is that what you're saying?


